            "hypothesis": "Tile path: fuse the base-sequence generation into the cooperative shared-memory staging so the tile block regenerates its 256+2*2 halo samples from the index in registers instead of loading them from a materialised global array. Mechanism: at the profiled size (n~8448, 33 blocks x 256 threads) the work per kernel is only a few thousand 5-tap folds, so the tile path was bound by (a) a second full grid launch (fill_base_kernel, 2.688 us of the 5.728 us median) and (b) the extra global store + reload round trip through L2 for data that is a pure function of the index. Removing the fill launch halves the kernel count and eliminates the dependent global traffic; the staging loop now issues only IMAD/LOP3 arithmetic plus conflict-free unit-stride shared stores. Genuine tiling is preserved: one __shared__ int[260] per block, cooperative halo staging, a single __syncthreads(), and each staged sample still reused by five threads through bank-conflict-free shared reads (lane t reads sh[t..t+4], so every unrolled tap touches consecutive banks). Geometry, masking of the out-of-range halos (staged as 0, reproducing the [0,n) truncation) and the gi<n tail guard are unchanged, and int indices stay well within range for n<=1e6. A free experiment confirmed bit-exact output vs the CPU oracle for n in {1,2,3,4,5,16,17,18,33,255,256,257,511,512,513,1000,8448,65535} and measured 3.949 us -> 1.890 us per iteration; a 512-wide tile with two adjacent outputs per thread and an int2 store was not faster (1.911 us), so the incumbent 256x256 tile geometry was kept. SIMT kernel source and launch branch untouched.",
